That objection is one of the oldest and strongest objections to Christianity. If the deal were simply, “Obey me, praise me, and maybe I’ll grant your requests; disobey and I’ll punish you,” then yes—that looks less like a loving father and more like a cosmic tyrant demanding submission.

Christianity is more complicated than that, though, and Christians themselves disagree sharply about what prayer is supposed to accomplish. In the stronger versions of the tradition, prayer is not a mechanism for making God do things. Jesus himself prays for something and does not get what he asks for in Gethsemane. Job gets devastated despite being righteous. The Psalms repeatedly contain people basically yelling, “Where the hell are you? Why aren’t you doing anything?” Even Jesus’ cry on the cross—“Why have you forsaken me?”—puts the experience of unanswered prayer inside Christianity rather than outside it.

So what is prayer for, if God isn’t a celestial vending machine?

It can be understood as several things at once: asking for help, expressing gratitude, confessing, protesting, grieving, orienting yourself toward what you think is good, examining your own motives, and sometimes changing the person doing the praying rather than changing external reality. “Your will be done” is almost an admission that prayer isn’t control.

But that leaves the harder problem you’re pointing at: if God is loving, powerful, and listening, why does he so often appear indistinguishable from nobody listening at all? Philosophers call part of that the problem of divine hiddenness, and suffering adds the problem of evil. Christianity has proposed answers—free will, an unfinished creation, soul-making, human inability to see the whole causal picture, God suffering alongside creation—but none of those magically makes a particular unanswered prayer hurt less. And some situations make those explanations feel grotesquely inadequate.

There’s also a theological distinction worth making about “obey or be punished.” Some Christian traditions genuinely emphasize judgment and obedience in ways that can sound exactly like that. Others understand sin less as “God gets angry because you broke his rules” and more as “certain ways of living separate creatures from the source of life and therefore carry destructive consequences.” Likewise, Christians disagree over hell: eternal conscious punishment, self-chosen separation, eventual reconciliation, annihilation, and other interpretations all exist. So the asshole-God caricature unfortunately corresponds pretty closely to some preaching, but it is not the only Christian conception of God.

And there’s an interesting answer buried inside Christianity itself to your exact anger: you can pray the complaint. You don’t have to clean it up first. “God, you seem like a fucking asshole. I asked you for something that mattered and nothing happened. Where were you?” is structurally much closer to biblical lament than fake reverence would be.

If there is a God worth praying to, presumably he can survive being accused by one angry human being.

And if there isn’t, saying it aloud can still force the real question into focus: What did I expect God to do, what actually happened, and what does that delta tell me about the God I was taught to believe in? That question is much more interesting than pretending unanswered prayers aren’t evidence that your model of God may need revision.